22:46:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you — and you’re certainly not alone in feeling that way. A lot of people, when they first hear about FOIA, assume (quite reasonably) that a law called the *Freedom of Information Act* would mean exactly what it sounds like: freedom to get any government record, no exceptions. So when they encounter redactions, it feels like a bait-and-switch. Let me engage directly with your point instead of just reciting the law. ### The name can be misleading if you take it literally You’re right: the title “Freedom of Information Act” is a bit of a slogan. Congress chose that name in 1966 specifically to signal a dramatic shift in government posture — from a default of secrecy to a presumption of openness. Before FOIA, agencies could basically withhold anything they wanted. FOIA flipped the burden: now an agency has to justify a withholding, and the public has a legal right to sue to enforce access. But even the most passionate advocates for open government in 1966 knew that a truly absolute “no exceptions” rule would be disastrous. So they baked in the nine exemptions right from the start. The name is aspirational; the text is a balancing act. ### What would “no exceptions” actually look like? Let’s test the “hand over whatever’s requested, no exceptions” standard in real life. Under that rule, any person could request — and the government would have to release, unredacted: - The exact blueprints of a military weapon system that gave American troops a tactical advantage. - The full name, home address, and personal details of every FBI informant who infiltrated a criminal organization. - The complete, unredacted medical records of a public figure who was briefly treated at a VA hospital, including all psychiatric notes, family history, and diagnoses. - Ongoing strategy memos where federal prosecutors lay out their next moves in a complex corruption case, including their weaknesses and the names of witnesses who haven’t yet testified. - The raw financial data that a pharmaceutical company was required to submit to the FDA, revealing the entire research pipeline a competitor could exploit. Very few people, if they sat with those examples, would actually be comfortable with all that information being available to anyone, anywhere, for any purpose — including hostile foreign governments, stalkers, criminals trying to eliminate witnesses, or business rivals. And those are exactly the kinds of things the exemptions protect. A law that forced disclosure in those cases would likely do more harm than good, and might soon be repealed or defanged by a panicked Congress after a disaster. ### The “freedom” in FOIA isn’t absolute — but neither are most freedoms We accept limits on free speech for things like defamation or true threats. We accept limits on the right to bear arms for certain classes of people and places. Those aren’t betrayals of the Constitution; they’re acknowledgments that even fundamental rights have boundaries when they collide with other important interests. FOIA works the same way: the public’s interest in access doesn’t automatically override the interest in a fair trial, personal privacy, or national security. The law picks a point on the spectrum, and that point is “as much disclosure as possible, with narrow, specific, challengeable exceptions.” ### The crucial safety valve: you can fight the redactions Where the “no exceptions” ethos does live on is in the process: if you think an agency is hiding behind an exemption that doesn’t actually fit, you can appeal, and if necessary, take the agency to federal court. Judges then review the withheld material in private and can order it released. That adversarial check is the mechanism meant to enforce the spirit of the law — to make sure agencies aren’t just redacting for convenience or embarrassment. So I completely understand your frustration. The name sets a high bar. But the reality is that the law was never intended to be a rule of absolute transparency. It’s a tool to maximize transparency while minimizing serious, foreseeable harms. Whether that balance is struck perfectly is a separate, very legitimate debate — but the very existence of redactions isn’t a violation of the concept; it’s part of the concept’s design.
