22:47:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I really appreciate your trust in your teacher, and it sounds like she was passionate about civics—that’s genuinely valuable. Let me offer a way to think about this that doesn’t just dismiss her or you. ### The likely source of the confusion High school civics classes, even very thorough ones, often **simplify** complex areas of law to convey the core principle. When your teacher said, “FOIA means citizens can get any government document they want,” she was almost certainly capturing the **spirit** of the law, not its literal letter. Before FOIA, the government routinely withheld documents without needing a reason. FOIA flipped that: now you have a right to *ask*, and the agency must give you the document unless a specific, narrow exemption applies. That’s a revolutionary shift. To a high schooler learning it for the first time, “You can now request any government document” is the big, headline message. But—and I’m sure your teacher knew this if she read the actual statute—the law’s text, from day one in 1966, contains the nine exemptions. Any detailed lesson plan covering FOIA for more than a single class period will eventually hit the exemptions. It’s possible that: - A time constraint meant the exemptions were glossed over. - She emphasized the general rule so much that the qualifier (“unless an exemption applies”) slipped away. - Or you might have remembered the bold, empowering part, which is completely natural. ### A quick reality check If I handed you a copy of the U.S. Code (5 U.S.C. § 552) right now, you could scroll down to subsection (b) and see the nine numbered exemptions, starting with “(b) This section does not apply to matters that are…” and then listing things like “(1) specifically authorized under criteria established by an Executive order to be kept secret in the interest of national defense or foreign policy…” That’s not interpretation; it’s black-letter law. Any FOIA response letter you receive from an agency will cite those exact exemption categories when they redact something. Courts have spent decades interpreting them, but the existence of the exemptions is undisputed. ### How to reconcile what you learned Instead of thinking “my teacher was wrong and the law is a sham,” you might reframe it as: **your teacher gave you the activist’s vision of FOIA—the one that inspired its creation.** She taught you the ideal that government information belongs to the people, subject to a presumption of openness. That ideal is real, and it’s the engine that drives requesters, journalists, and public interest groups to use FOIA to uncover so much. The exemptions are the practical guardrails that the same Congress, made up of people who believed in that ideal, felt were necessary to prevent serious harm. A full civics education would include both parts: the broad right, and the limited but necessary carve‑outs. If your teacher only taught the first part, she gave you an incomplete but not entirely false picture—more like the headline than the whole article. ### What you can do with this You don’t have to just take my word (or hers). The next time you’re curious about FOIA, you can: - Read the exemptions for yourself on a site like FOIA.gov or in the statute. - Watch how an agency’s denial letter cites specific exemptions. - See how judges handle FOIA lawsuits—they don’t debate whether exemptions exist, only whether the agency applied them correctly. I’m not here to dunk on a teacher who clearly inspired you to care about open government. But I’d gently suggest that if she really insisted there are **no exceptions at all**, she might have oversimplified a subject that even lawyers and judges continue to wrestle with. The truth is somewhere in the middle: FOIA is a powerful right, but it was never designed to be absolute.
