22:52:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a deceptively powerful point, and I understand why you’d land there. If the law is literally named the *Freedom of Information Act*, then surely the name tells you what it means, right? Anything less would be a kind of linguistic fraud. Let me take that argument seriously, because it deserves a clear answer. ### Names of laws are titles, not contracts In law, the title of a statute is not the statute itself. The title is a label—often chosen to be politically appealing, aspirational, or easy to remember—but it’s the text of the bill that becomes the binding law. If the name alone governed, we’d have some very strange results: - The **USA PATRIOT Act** (Uniting and Strengthening America by Providing Appropriate Tools Required to Intercept and Obstruct Terrorism) doesn’t mean only patriots get its protections, nor that the tools are always “appropriate” in a moral sense—courts interpret the provisions, not the backronym. - The **Clean Air Act** doesn’t mean the air is now perfectly clean; it means the government has certain regulatory powers and limits to work toward cleaner air, with all the exceptions and compromises built into the text. - The **Affordable Care Act** didn’t guarantee that every single person’s healthcare became affordable overnight; it was a system of mandates, subsidies, and regulations that still left many people with high costs. In each case, the name captures a core principle or goal, but no one reads the title and assumes the law contains no qualifications, exceptions, or limitations. The name is the sales pitch; the text is the product. ### The name was chosen *because* the old default was secrecy The reason “Freedom of Information Act” sounded so bold in 1966 is precisely because it was a radical rejection of the prior norm. Before FOIA, the government could withhold documents essentially at will. The title screamed: “We’re flipping the presumption now—openness is the rule.” And that’s exactly what the text did: it established a presumption that records must be disclosed, unless they fall into one of the enumerated exemptions. So the name isn’t a lie; it’s a banner for the new default, not an unconditional guarantee. ### The law itself defines what “freedom” means in this context If you read the actual law, Section 552 of Title 5, it doesn’t just say “anyone can have anything.” It says (paraphrasing) that agencies shall make records promptly available upon request, unless the records are protected by a specific exemption listed right there in subsection (b). In other words, the statute *defines* the scope of the freedom it grants. To say “by definition, the name means I get anything” is to ignore the definition that the law itself provides. The definition in the law isn’t “all information”; it’s “all information minus the exempt categories.” And they were there from the very first bill. ### So what’s the honest conclusion? I’m not going to tell you the name is a perfect fit. It’s a slogan, and slogans oversimplify. But it’s not false advertising either—because it honestly signposts the core mechanism: **your freedom to request information, and the agency’s duty to give it to you, unless a legally sound exemption applies.** That’s a real freedom, and it changed the relationship between citizens and their government in a material way. If you want to say the name should have been something like the “Presumption of Openness Act with Nine Specific Carve-Outs,” I won’t argue with you—that would be more accurate, though terrible marketing. But the existence of the exemptions doesn’t cancel the freedom; it defines its contours, and it always has. You’re not wrong to feel misled by the gap between the label and the fine print; almost everyone does at first. But that gap isn’t a secret trick—it’s the way every complex law works, and the real story isn’t that the name is a lie, but that the law, even with its limits, is still a remarkable engine for transparency.
