22:54:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: This is your most important question yet, because it’s not just about what the law *says*—it’s about what happens when bureaucracies, over time, are allowed to interpret their own rules. You’re describing the possibility of a slow-motion hollowing out, and that is a genuinely serious concern. Let me take it head-on. ### Yes, exemption creep is a real danger—and it’s a known one You’re not imagining things. Legal scholars, transparency advocates, and even some judges have warned that agencies can drift toward overusing the deliberative process privilege. The temptation is real: why risk releasing a draft that could generate a bad headline, when you can just stamp it “pre-decisional” and avoid the fight? Over decades, without pushback, that instinct could theoretically expand until almost nothing internal sees daylight. If that happened, you’d be right: the disclosure mandate would become meaningless in practice. ### But here’s why it hasn’t happened—and why the trend actually runs the other way The legal and political ecosystem has multiple brakes that actively squeeze the exemption over time, rather than letting it balloon. The direction of travel since 1966 has been **toward more disclosure, not less**, and the deliberative privilege is narrower today than it was fifty years ago. Here’s why: **1. Court decisions have steadily narrowed the exemption.** Every time an agency loses a FOIA case over Exemption 5, that loss becomes binding precedent. Courts have ruled, for example, that: - Purely factual material within a deliberative document must be segregated and released. You can’t hide data just because it’s in a draft memo. - Documents that are adopted as the agency’s final working law or policy lose their “pre-decisional” character and become disclosable. - The privilege only protects documents that are both pre-decisional *and* deliberative. A calendar showing who met with whom, for instance, is factual and not protected. A final report summarizing a decision is not protected, even if it discusses the process. - The government’s interest in confidentiality must be weighed against the public interest in disclosure, and where there is evidence of government misconduct, the public interest often wins. Every published opinion like this shrinks the zone of permissible withholding. So the cumulative effect of the “years later” slap-downs is a legal landscape that becomes increasingly unfavorable to the agency’s broadest claims. **2. Congress has tightened the law explicitly to combat this.** In 2016, Congress passed the **FOIA Improvement Act**, which added a crucial new requirement: the “foreseeable harm” standard. Now, even if a document falls within an exemption, the agency can only withhold it if it “reasonably foresees that disclosure would harm an interest protected by such exemption.” In other words, it’s no longer enough to say “this is deliberative”; the agency must now show a real, concrete risk of harm from release. That was a direct response to the fear you just articulated—that agencies were reflexively withholding without evaluating the actual damage. **3. Proactive disclosure and the “release to one, release to all” norm change the default.** The same 2016 law pushes agencies to post frequently requested records online. Once a document has been released in a FOIA lawsuit (even years later), it becomes publicly available to everyone, forever. The agency knows that a future loss doesn’t just resolve one case—it creates permanent public access to that type of record. That prospect discourages stretching the exemption too far, because the cost of losing is a permanent window you can’t close again. **4. Internal culture and external watchdogs make overreach costly.** Agency FOIA officers and general counsel are now part of a professional community that understands the law’s direction. Deliberately stretching the exemption invites not just lawsuits, but inspector general audits, congressional inquiries, and negative press. While culture is not a foolproof guard, it’s a real one: the instinct to cover up has a counter-instinct among legal professionals who don’t want to be seen as obstructionists. ### The crucial distinction: delay vs. denial You’re absolutely right that a right delayed can feel like a right denied, and that’s a legitimate failing of the current system. But delay is not the same as a legal vacuum where “anything goes.” The eventual enforceability exerts a forward-looking pressure on what agencies attempt. If a category of records is consistently ruled non-exempt after a fight, agencies adapt and stop withholding that category. Over the long arc, the definition of “deliberative” has been chiseled into a more precise, narrower shape—not because agencies voluntarily shrank it, but because they lost in court enough times that the boundaries hardened. So, could an agency, at this moment, try to stretch the term “deliberative” further than the courts would allow? Yes. Might it take a couple of years for a challenge to correct them? Yes. But has the system, over time, allowed them to “basically classify almost everything that way” and make the disclosure rule meaningless? No. The trend is the opposite. The exemption is a well-defined, tested legal doctrine, not a play-dough term, and the agencies that try to stretch it too far eventually create the very precedents that handcuff them and every other agency in the future. Your fear is a warning about the need for perpetual vigilance. It’s not a description of where the law stands today. And the fact that you’re asking these questions is, in a small but real way, part of the vigilance that keeps the system from rotting into the meaningless shell you’re worried about.
