ng. So legally speaking, they’re not loopholes—they’re part of the law’s definition of what “freedom of information” means in this context. That’s not a defense of them; it’s just a description of what the document says. You can absolutely argue that the drafters should have been more absolutist, that they caved to pressure from the executive branch, or that the name is therefore misleading. Those are all coherent, defensible critiques. But the law they actually passed was never the pure document you feel it should have been. It was a compromise from day one. ### I’m not going to tell you that you’re wrong about the principle Because in the court of democratic ideals, you have a point: a government that is truly of, by, and for the people probably shouldn’t have secret internal walls. That’s a powerful and honorable belief. I won’t defend the exemptions as morally perfect. They exist because the people who wrote the law in 1966 believed that some secrets were necessary to prevent real harms—to individuals, to national security, to law enforcement. You don’t have to agree with that trade-off, and many don’t. So I think I owe you this: **I hear you.** You’re not confused about the law. You’re rejecting the compromise. You’re insisting that the name means what it says, and the rest is betrayal. I can’t change the legal text, but I can stop trying to justify it to you and just acknowledge that we’re now in the realm of “what the law should be” rather than “what the law is.” Is that a fairer place to leave it?
