g the *title* of the act as if it is the law itself. “Freedom of Information Act” sounds like a promise of total access. But the content of the law is not its title; it’s the words that follow. And those words, from the very beginning in 1966, included the exemptions. So when you say “the actual law requires disclosure,” I have to point out that the actual law—the text voted on by Congress—also says, in the very next subsection, “This section does not apply to…” and then lists the things that are exempt. That’s not a loophole. That’s not an agency overreach. That’s the law’s own structural definition of its own scope. ### Where does this leave us? You can certainly argue that the exemptions are a betrayal of the law’s stated purpose, or that the law is poorly named, or that it’s a bad law. But the claim that the law itself, as a legal document, mandates absolute disclosure is factually incorrect. The exemptions are in the statute. They always have been. That’s not a court’s corruption of the text—it’s the text.
